%N A399580 Table read by antidiagonals: T(n,k) = number of maximum blackouts of the n X k grid of points, where a blackout deletes points so that every rectangle with all four vertices on grid points remains uniquely identifiable from its visible vertices, and maximum blackouts are those of size A397315(n,k); n, k >= 2.
%C A399580 Companion to A397315, which gives the blackout sizes.
%C A399580 A blackout S is a set of grid points removed. The visible vertices of a rectangle R are the corners of R not in S. "Uniquely identifiable" means that no two distinct rectangles have the same set of visible vertices; equivalently, the map R -> C(R) \ S is injective, where C(R) is the set of corners of R. A rectangle may have no visible vertices, but injectivity implies at most one rectangle can be fully hidden.
%C A399580 T(5,5) = 8: the maximum blackout of the 5 X 5 grid is unique up to the symmetries of the square.
%C A399580 From _Arjun Pemmasani_, Oct 30 2026: (Start)
%C A399580 Row 3 (with k >= 2) is A401223, row 4 (with k >= 2) is A401224, and row 5 for k >= 8 is A401225.
%C A399580 For k >= 2 the number of maximum blackouts of the 2 X k grid counted up to the symmetries of the rectangle is A322284(k-1).
%C A399580 For k >= 6 every maximum blackout of the 4 X k grid has four columns with two points each and one point in every other column, and the four row pairs are the edges of a 4-cycle on the rows. For k >= 8 every maximum blackout of the 5 X k grid has six columns with two points each and one point in every other column, and the six row pairs are the edges of a complete bipartite graph K_{2,3} on the rows.
%C A399580 For each fixed n the g.f. of row n is rational (see the Pemmasani link).
%C A399580 T(7,8) >= 18: one SAT enumeration lists 18 maximum blackouts of the 7 X 8 grid (where A397315(7,8) = 16), and no second count has finished. (End)
%H A399580 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="/A399580/b399580.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..67</a> (antidiagonals 4..14 of the table, flattened)
%H A399580 Peter Kagey, <a href="https://peterkagey.com/problems/001/">Open Problem Collection, Problem 001</a>.
%H A399580 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/blob/main/kagey-problems/problem001/jis/pemmasani.pdf">Maximum Blackouts That Keep Every Lattice Rectangle Identifiable</a>, preprint, 2026.
%H A399580 Arjun Pemmasani, <a href="https://github.com/apemm/Kagey-Problems/tree/main/kagey-problems/problem001">Solvers, exhaustive verification, and pre-registration ledger for Problem 001</a> (GitHub repository).
%F A399580 T(2,m) = m*2^(m-1) for m >= 3 (Strip Theorem, see link).
%F A399580 From _Arjun Pemmasani_, Oct 30 2026: (Start)
%F A399580 T(n,k) = T(k,n).
%F A399580 T(3,k) = 4*(k-1)*(3*k+2)*3^(k-5) + A398448(k) for k >= 4.
%F A399580 T(3,k) ~ (4/81)*k^2*3^k.
%F A399580 T(4,k) ~ c*k^4*r^k with r = 3.3519967773103758... and c = 0.0000816109707754112... (see A401224). (End)
